Problem Overview

We're given an RSA-encrypted message, but instead of the usual two primes, the modulus n is composed of multiple primes (hence "ClusterRSA").

Given:
text

n = 8749002899132047699790752490331099938058737706735201354674975134719667510377522805717156720453193651
e = 65537
ct = 1540727604168001476834564078802265850886645733701728735298015222241133874453234034740567290095334786

Step 1: Understanding Multi-prime RSA

In standard RSA:

    n = p × q (2 primes)

    φ(n) = (p-1)(q-1)

    d = e⁻¹ mod φ(n)

In multi-prime RSA:

    n = p₁ × p₂ × p₃ × ... × pₖ (k primes)

    φ(n) = (p₁-1)(p₂-1)(p₃-1)...(pₖ-1)

    d = e⁻¹ mod φ(n) (same formula!)

The decryption process is identical once we have all the prime factors.
Step 2: Factoring n

Since n is 100 digits long (~330 bits), manual factoring is impossible. We use FactorDB, an online database of known factorizations.

